.th talloc I August/75
.sh NAME
talloc \*- manage ownership of mag tapes
.sh SYNOPSIS
talloc [option] [tapename]
.sh DESCRIPTION
This program maintains a file of free and allocated mag tapes.
If you need a mag tape, you may use this program to give you the name
of an available tape.  The program will record the fact that you own the
tape.  The various forms of the command follow:
.sp
talloc -- Will print the name of a free tape, if one is available, and will
prompt you for a comment on the use of the tape.
.sp
talloc tapename -- Will allocate the requested tapename to you if no one else
owns it.  If you already own it, it will prompt you for a change in the
comment associated with the tape.
.sp
talloc -f tapename -- Will free "tapename" for whoever wants it.
.sp
talloc -p[u] -- Will print the tape file sorted by tapename, unless the "u"
is appended, in which case the sorting is by username.
.sp 2
This program enforces only one access at a time to the tape file, so
you may be told to try again later.  The program runs as user "bin", which user
also owns the tape file.
If you are logged on as the root, you are allowed to allocate and free
anyones tapes, so be careful.
.sh FILES
/usr/adm/tfile - The file of tapes
.br
/usr/adm/tfilelck - The file used to enforce single access at a time
.br
/usr/adm/newtfile - Temporarily created to hold the updated tfile
.sh AUTHOR
Mike Tilson
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
Numerous
.sh BUGS
It needs to have the entire tape file in core, which will cause trouble
if we ever get a whole lot of tapes someday.  (It should handle
50 or 60 just fine now.)
